     [38;5;153mformatDuration[39m, with 13 rendering tests covering everything in the spec's testing section.
  2. [1mRoute and nav[0m ([38;5;153msrc/server.js[39m, [38;5;153msrc/layout.js[39m, [38;5;153mtest/server.test.js[39m): the [38;5;153m/deploys[39m route, the nav link after Services,
     and server tests for a 200 response and for a 503 when [38;5;153mdeploys.json[39m is missing or contains broken JSON.

  [1mMain design choice:[0m the page is built entirely from the existing [38;5;153msrc/ui/[39m components. They already cover everything it
  needs: the header, the filter dropdown that submits on change and keeps the current sort, sortable headers with ▲/▼,
  the colored chips and the empty state. The page itself only checks the query values, filters the rows, maps each
  status to a chip color and formats durations. The plan says explicitly [3mnot[0m to copy [38;5;153msrc/pages/services.js[39m, which builds
  its own form, table, [38;5;153mpill-*[39m classes and escaping. So the change needs no new CSS and no edits to the vendored
  [38;5;153msrc/ui/[39m.

  [1mDecisions the spec leaves open, listed in the plan so you can overrule them:[0m
  - An unknown [38;5;153mdir[39m falls back to the sorted column's natural direction: Started goes newest first, Service goes A→Z.
  - When sorted by Service, deploys of the same service stay newest first.
  - Deploys longer than an hour stay in minutes, e.g. "72m 5s".
  - "All environments" with no deploys at all says just "No deploys".

